# Verify chat and account isolation on Android

Use a disposable Android API 30 or later phone emulator. Install the debug app and its instrumentation APK. The journey uses two temporary Hermes runtimes backed by the fork’s loopback model fixture. It creates private test accounts, sends chat and code requests, applies a layout proposed by the model, and restores the workspace.

Run the following from the repository root in the fork’s test environment. Replace the checkout and emulator serial with your own.

```sh
python verification/live_api.py --source /path/to/clean/hermes-agent --require-tool-controls \
    --device-command python verification/device/run.py --serial emulator-5554 \
    --output /tmp/hermes-mobile-captures
```

Add `--adb-port` to the device command when the emulator uses a dedicated ADB server. The driver creates its own loopback port reversals and removes them on exit. Each run uses fresh local passphrases. It checks both the server-provided model inventory and the handset’s private thread state.

The first device phase verifies chat, a code artifact, agent layout apply and undo, model settings, usage, reminder creation, pause, resume and deletion. It opens the software keyboard and sets a private draft. A second user sees an empty history, and that user’s passphrase fails to open the first account. The second phase force-stops and relaunches the app, unlocks the original account and checks the restored draft with a 1.5× font scale in night mode. It removes the two synthetic accounts once both phases pass. The host restores the emulator’s original night mode, font scale and keyboard settings even when a check fails.

Snapshots are drawn inside the test process at the activity viewport dimensions. Visible application windows are drawn in stacking order at their actual positions, including the dimmed backdrop behind a dialog. Android’s private-window flag stays enabled. The keyboard-visible capture includes the resized application at the time the input method reports itself visible. Measuring actual push delivery after ordinary process death requires a push-capable handset or emulator and a configured provider.

The live-workspace version adds a third phase after the restart check. A fresh account opens a coding discussion, disables and restores the individual `write_file` tool, and verifies the fixture model’s reply for each setting. In another discussion the model returns proposals for a named page and a custom composer layout. The journey applies both through the artifact reader, stages a form response beside an existing draft, returns to the page, and explicitly sends the draft. It also checks that the secondary account sees an empty gallery, that the original account recovers its page after unlocking, and that restoring the layout removes the page from the gallery.

The additional capture files are `12-formulaire-brouillon.png`, `13-ecran-personnel.png`, and `14-outil-discussion.png`. The same two gateway processes can run the proposal, transcript, tool-policy, and isolation checks without a device by omitting `--device-command` and using `--require-tool-controls`. The JSON report identifies the proposal surfaces, the number of verified turns, and the two tool-dependent replies.

The required Android device check also exercises six conversation turns against the same pinned Hermes source. It runs the HTTP contract before booting the emulator, then drives the installed app through a choice, a typed answer, a dictated answer, a denial, a one-time approval and a second denial for the same action. The app reopens the original question and answer after locking. A synthetic recognizer supplies editable text, and the gateway keeps the question pending until the user taps Send.

The `android-conversation-cards` CI artifact contains the HTTP and Android receipts and eight original screenshots. The Android receipt records the installed app and test APK digests, the image digests and the canonical prompt identities and decisions. Partial screenshots remain available when a device assertion fails.

To run the cards locally with already-built APKs, choose a fresh output directory for each run.

```sh
python verification/conversation_cards.py --source /path/to/clean/hermes-agent \
    --output /tmp/hermes-cards-http
python verification/conversation_cards.py --source /path/to/clean/hermes-agent \
    --serial emulator-5554 --output /tmp/hermes-cards-android \
    --verify-apks app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk \
    app/build/outputs/apk/androidTest/debug/app-debug-androidTest.apk
```

The thread-instrument journey uses the same installed APKs and pinned gateway checkout to exercise a model-authored tool inside one discussion. On the compact screen, the user edits the creation request, tries the proposed form, and checks the draft it produces. They compare a revision, adopt it and undo it. After the app restarts, the wide screen shows the discussion and its pinned form side by side. A second conversation checks that the pin stays with its owner.

Download and extract the `android-thread-instruments` artifact. Open `walkthrough.html` in Chrome to follow the seven key steps offline. The walkthrough describes each step in English and preserves the French Android screenshots. Select a step or use the left and right arrow keys. The artifact also contains the full set of original phone captures, including separate views of the pinned and proposed revisions. Each image has a French caption, dimensions and a SHA-256 digest in `thread-instrument.json`. The receipt also identifies both installed APKs and the four completed gateway runs across the two conversations. If an assertion fails, CI retains the screenshots collected before the failure. The eight conversation-card images and their receipts remain available in `android-conversation-cards`.

To rebuild the standalone walkthrough from a completed receipt and its original screenshots, run `python verification/instrument_walkthrough.py /path/to/evidence` from the repository root. Verification checks every capture against the receipt before embedding the selected steps.

To repeat the instrument journey, use the same fork environment and a fresh output directory.

```sh
python verification/thread_instruments.py --source /path/to/clean/hermes-agent \
    --serial emulator-5554 --output /tmp/hermes-thread-instrument \
    --verify-apks app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk \
    app/build/outputs/apk/androidTest/debug/app-debug-androidTest.apk
```

## Photos and skills in a discussion

The hosted device job uses the packaged APK pair for both journeys. It opens the Android photo picker, removes and reselects a generated image, and sends it from the protected draft. The fixture model checks every decoded pixel before returning its scripted answer. The conversation list must show the original caption. A second fixture browses the authenticated skills catalog, searches for a skill, opens its summary, and stages its qualified name alongside existing draft text. Before Android taps **Send**, the host checks that the gateway has admitted zero runs. After the tap it checks the saved conversation, actual skill load and final answer. The Android journey also opens the compact tool result and checks that the full skill text is selectable.

The **android-discussion-photo-skills** artifact contains the two photo captures, four skills captures and their receipts. Successful receipts include the installed APK digests and capture digests. The fixtures create disposable profiles and use synthetic content.
